# bd writes those files and says nothing about where work starts, so the
# project's own declaration goes in here. Between markers, because bd writes
# them again on setup, doctor --fix and upgrades, and this has to survive that.
MARK_OPEN = "<!-- dotdiw: how work starts -->"
MARK_CLOSE = "<!-- /dotdiw -->"
CONVENTION_FILES = ("AGENTS.md", "CLAUDE.md")

# Function words only would not do here: this block is the one place the two
# conventions are stated to whoever shows up, so it names the command, the file
# suffix, and why a formula beats a runbook.
# Every adopted checkout rewrites its copy of this on its next create, as a change
# in its own tree, and two versions in one team rewrite each other's. So a word
# here changes last: it still says mol pour, which still works.
CONVENTIONS = """%(open)s
## How work starts in this project

Start every task and subtask with:

    %(p)s create "<what you are about to do>"

That is the convention, and it is also what lets the right procedure find you.
On `create`, the procedures this project holds are matched against your title
and the ones that apply are printed. Start by cutting a branch or editing a
file instead and nothing surfaces -- the discipline was skipped, not the
mechanism broken.

A procedure you repeat belongs in a formula, not a runbook: a TOML file named
`<something>.formula.toml`, kept next to whatever it is about, and committed.
Prose cannot be matched against a title or instantiated as steps; a formula is
both. Leave it where it belongs in the tree -- it does not need moving or
linking anywhere.

Write one the moment you notice you are repeating yourself, or that you just
did by hand what you will do again. `formula new` writes the file for you; you
fill in the description and the steps.

Then commit it. A formula you have not committed is one only you hold: it is
found on your file system alone, nobody else's `create` reaches it, and `check`
says so. Committing it is the last step of writing it, not a separate decision
-- and this is the project asking you to, so it is not the kind of commit a
conservative git policy is about. Commit the formula file; leave the rest of
your changes as they are.

    %(p)s formula new "<what the procedure is for>"
    %(p)s formula new "..." --from <id>   steps from work already done
    %(p)s formula list     the procedures this project holds
    %(p)s mol pour <id>    instantiate one as tracked, ordered steps
    %(p)s check            whether every formula here can actually be found
